{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the cobblestones like nature’s confetti. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, the bitterness balanced by sweet wagashi, and afternoons wandering through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, each one a silent prayer.\n\nTokyo was a sensory explosion. Shibuya’s scramble crossing felt like a choreographed dance of a thousand souls. I ate ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, slurping noodles so hot they made my eyes water—perfect. In Akihabara, I wandered past glowing anime posters and retro game arcades, feeling like I’d stepped into a living manga.\n\nThe bullet train to Hiroshima was silent, smooth, and astonishingly punctual. At the Peace Memorial Park, I stood before the Atomic Bomb Dome, humbled by silence where once there was chaos. A single origami crane, left by a child, rested on the memorial’s edge—I tucked one into my pocket as I left.\n\nNara’s deer were the unexpected stars. They bowed for senbei, nudged my hand gently, and followed me like loyal pets. I laughed as one stole my snack, its velvet nose brushing my palm.\n\nAt night, I stayed in a ryokan in Hakone. Tatami mats, sliding paper doors, and a private onsen under the stars—hot water steaming, mountains shadowed in moonlight. I drifted off to the sound of a distant koto.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me beauty—it taught me stillness. In the bow of a shopkeeper, the precision of a bento box, the way a temple bell echoed then faded—I learned to slow down. To notice. To be present.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of matcha, a journal full of sketches, and a heart full of quiet gratitude. I didn’t just visit Japan—I remembered how to breathe.\n\n(Word count: 298)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.45808762311935425}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2194